
The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy Jr.
Secretary of Health and Human Services

Dear Secretary Kennedy:

My name is Alexander Ferrari Miller. I am an adult with disabilities who lives in a licensed room-and-board residence in California. I have Type 2 diabetes and rely on the meals provided by the residence.

The staff members do their best to provide three meals each day, and I appreciate their efforts. However, because of the limitations of the current system, many residents with medical conditions cannot consistently obtain foods that are appropriate for their health needs.

Current policy generally prevents residents of room-and-board facilities from receiving Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP) benefits. As a result, I cannot independently purchase the foods my medical condition requires, even when those foods would supplement, rather than replace, the meals provided by the residence.

For a person with diabetes, healthier choices often include high-fiber cereals instead of sugary cereals, whole-wheat bread instead of white bread, brown or wild rice instead of white rice, lean chicken breast, fish, plain yogurt, kefir, fermented beverages such as kombucha, fresh vegetables, nuts, popcorn without excessive butter, and sugar-free beverages such as Coke Zero. These are examples rather than a complete list. The broader principle is that people with diabetes need access to medically appropriate nutrition.

I respectfully ask your department to examine whether existing law, including the Americans with Disabilities Act where applicable, can be used to provide reasonable accommodations for people with disabilities who live in room-and-board facilities. My hope is that individuals with documented medical conditions would be permitted to receive SNAP benefits, or another comparable accommodation, to supplement the meals already provided by their residences.

I am not asking for duplicate benefits or unnecessary expenditures. I am asking for a practical accommodation that allows people with disabilities to follow medically appropriate diets without disrupting the existing room-and-board system.

Thank you for your consideration of this issue and for your service to the American people.

Respectfully,

Alexander Ferrari Miller